  "summary": "Round 6's critique correctly identified the rejection-handling contradiction, the FeatureFile method vs standalone function issue, and the WebUI relay misunderstanding — but its proposed fixes introduce new problems. Keeping rejected IDs in depends_on permanently (to support re-planning triggers) means is_blocked() must distinguish between 'resolved-by-done' and 'resolved-by-rejection' without any field to encode that distinction, since the only signal is the dependency's current stage — which works fine, making the 'don't remove' rule correct but for the wrong reason. The real issue is that round 6 conflates two separate concerns: dependency resolution (is the blocker out of the way?) and plan validity (does the downstream plan still make sense?). These should be decoupled: is_blocked(feature, all_features) checks whether all depends_on IDs map to features in terminal stages ({done, rejected}), while a separate plan_invalidation_check() — triggered only on rejection transitions — scans dependents and moves those in impl_stages back to plan-inbox. The depends_on list is never mutated by stage transitions; it's a static declaration of intent edited only by users. On the standalone-function design: round 6 is right that FeatureFile has no board context, but the proposed is_blocked(feature, all_features) signature is awkward at every call site because the caller must always pass the full list. A better pattern is a lightweight DependencyGraph class initialized once per auto-run cycle from the already-loaded feature list — it builds an id-to-stage dict and an adjacency list in O(n) time, then exposes is_blocked(feature_id) and has_cycle(feature_id, proposed_deps) as O(m) lookups. This avoids both the FeatureFile-method problem and the repetitive parameter-passing problem, and the graph object naturally scopes to a single board. On WebUI integration: the correct flow is (1) api.go HTTP handler validates and sends a WebSocket message {type: 'edit_depends_on', feature_id, depends_on: [...]} to the TUI client, (2) TUI's message dispatch adds a handler that calls feature.set_depends_on() and saves, (3) next state_update push reflects the change. This is three touch points, not one endpoint. On persistence: agree with round 6 that depends_on should only be written when non-empty to avoid schema-creep diffs — use a conditional in save() that omits the key when the list is empty, and default to [] on read via .get('depends_on', [])."
